                                               \nBased on my web search, here's a comprehensive overview of Claude Code CLI clear command behavior and session management:\n\n## /clear Command Behavior\n\n### What /clear Does:\n- **Clears conversation history** from Claude's context window\n- **Frees up context** by removing previous messages\n- **Starts fresh** for new tasks\n- **Can also be invoked as /reset or /new**\n\n### Session Management:\n- **Creates a new session** instead of clearing in-place (according to GitHub issues)\n- **Maintains project memory** while providing a fresh start\n- **Wipes the current conversation entirely** from the active session\n- **Keeps session files** for potential resuming later\n\n### Key Behaviors:\n1. **Context Reset**: Removes all conversation history and frees context\n2. **Performance Improvement**: Helps with long sessions that may have accumulated irrelevant context\n3. **Task Boundaries**: Best used to reset between unrelated tasks\n4. **Session Persistence**: Unlike manual deletion, /clear doesn't delete the session file - it just clears the conversation within it\n\n### Related Session Commands:\n- **/resume**: Return to or fork an earlier conversation\n- **/branch**: Create a fork from an earlier conversation point\n- **/list**: List available sessions\n- **Manual deletion**: Navigate to ~/.claude/ to delete .jsonl files\n\n### Issues and Considerations:\n- Some users report that /clear creates a new session with a multi-second delay\n- There are feature requests for better visual feedback when clearing\n- Users sometimes prefer starting a new session instead of using /clear for \"cleaner\" behavior\n- Session files accumulate over time, requiring manual cleanup\n\n### Best Practices:\n- Use /clear when you need to reset context between unrelated tasks\n- If you've corrected Claude more than twice on similar issues, consider clearing\n- Manual session deletion may be needed for old, test, or sensitive sessions\n- The command helps maintain performance by preventing overly long conversation histories\n\nThis gives you a clear picture of how the /clear command works within Claude Code's session management system and the various options available for managing your conversations and sessions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]}
